/-!
# The marked Mathieu input to type-J₄ recognition

The original closure is the full centralizer two-core and is extraspecial of
order 2¹³. Even characteristic makes that core self-centralizing, identifying
it with the generalized Fitting subgroup. The previously constructed distinct
L-conjugate supplies fusion in this very core. The only additional input is
the full cubic quotient from 8.2.13.

This module constructs the original type-J₄ hypotheses and applies the source
module and cubic-centralizer conclusions of AS91 (4.1), together with the
two distinct core-involution classes and their literal quotient-stabilizer
orders and both S₅/E₃₂ and S₅/E₁₆ structures from (4.2), the reciprocal
returning-core intersection, exact two-core image and returning fusion class
from (4.3), and the elementary three-space exactly generated by three
conjugate markers, with the exact same marked inputs. The cubic commutator
and seven-core normalizer construction now also give the actual full actions,
N_G(Z)=KS and K/(K∩S)≅L₃(2) from (4.4). The core centralizers
now construct an order-2¹⁵ subgroup of O₂(K)=O₂(R), containing [K,S]
in that two-core, with every nonzero point-core centralizer of order 2¹¹.
The special radical now has exact order 2¹⁵, center and derived/Frattini
subgroup Z, elementary center quotient of rank twelve, and literal S/R≃S₅.
The tensor action, ambient
recognition theorem and its application remain to be proved; no recognition
callback or exclusion of J₄ is used here.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, 8.2.12–14, printed pp.722–723; Aschbacher–Segev,
*The uniqueness of groups of type J₄*, Invent. Math. 105 (1991), p.589.
-/

